WEBVTT

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:04.872
Imagine teaching a robot to put a cup on a shelf In a simulator each

00:00:04.884 --> 00:00:08.804
attempt begins with the cup conveniently back on the table That convenience

00:00:08.816 --> 00:00:13.367
is doing real work Somebody or some code with extraordinary powers is

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:17.204
reversing the previous attempt Remove that reset button and the next

00:00:17.215 --> 00:00:20.992
attempt begins wherever the cup actually is Learning must now create

00:00:21.004 --> 00:00:25.424
its own opportunities to practice Draw the physical state as a dot moving

00:00:25.436 --> 00:00:29.392
along a continuous curve A training script may divide that curve into

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:33.752
episodes but drawing a vertical line does not move the dot A controller

00:00:33.764 --> 00:00:37.827
switch also leaves the dot where it is An external reset is different

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.616
it intervenes on the state often using a person or a mechanism outside

00:00:42.628 --> 00:00:46.477
the learned action space Our central question is therefore larger than

00:00:46.488 --> 00:00:50.706
maximizing reward on one attempt How does an agent make progress while

00:00:50.718 --> 00:00:55.341
preserving useful future attempts We will track task time recovery time

00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:59.391
and human intervention This series builds the necessary mathematics

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:03.859
then examines five approaches Every numerical experiment in the companion

00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:08.005
is a transparent teaching example rather than a reproduction of a paper's

00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:08.613
benchmark

00:01:10.615 --> 00:01:14.379
First distinguish three boundaries Termination says the return we have

00:01:14.391 --> 00:01:18.394
defined has ended If a task pays once when the cup reaches the shelf

00:01:18.561 --> 00:01:23.339
and then ends that success can be terminal A failure can also be terminal

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:27.556
if the task specification says so Terminal is a property of the modeled

00:01:27.568 --> 00:01:32.084
task not a synonym for an inconvenient observation Truncation says we

00:01:32.096 --> 00:01:35.753
stopped collecting this segment Perhaps a rollout buffer filled or a

00:01:35.765 --> 00:01:40.245
training timer expired If the underlying task continues the unobserved

00:01:40.257 --> 00:01:44.641
future still contributes value A time limit can instead be part of the

00:01:44.713 --> 00:01:49.133
actual task such as finishing within thirty seconds We must decide which

00:01:49.145 --> 00:01:53.303
interpretation is intended before choosing a learning target A handoff

00:01:53.374 --> 00:01:57.258
is a third event The forward controller yields to a recovery controller

00:01:57.532 --> 00:02:01.476
but the world keeps evolving That handoff need not terminate either

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:05.622
controller's counterfactual goal-reaching value In an off-policy learner

00:02:05.896 --> 00:02:10.078
we can collect with one behavior and evaluate another policy Keep these

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.938
three labels separate in your replay data a single undifferentiated

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.701
done flag throws away meaning

00:02:17.653 --> 00:02:21.882
Let the discounted return be the reward now plus gamma times the next

00:02:21.894 --> 00:02:26.588
reward plus gamma squared times the following reward and so on Factor

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.794
one gamma out of everything after the first reward The entire future

00:02:30.806 --> 00:02:35.154
becomes gamma times the next return This simple rearrangement is the

00:02:35.166 --> 00:02:39.193
source of the Bellman equation it is not an arbitrary training convention

00:02:39.586 --> 00:02:43.411
Now condition on the current state and on following a policy The conditional

00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:47.235
expectation of that future return is the value of the successor state

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.286
Because we rarely know the expectation exactly a temporal difference

00:02:51.298 --> 00:02:55.909
update uses a sampled reward and an estimated successor value The result

00:02:56.028 --> 00:03:00.341
is a bootstrap target observed evidence now plus an estimate of what

00:03:00.353 --> 00:03:04.761
remains If the modeled return truly ends the remaining contribution

00:03:04.952 --> 00:03:09.324
is zero Otherwise it remains present even when a data collector stops

00:03:09.336 --> 00:03:13.613
writing observations This gives the termination mask on screen Notice

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:17.986
what is absent neither a buffer boundary nor a controller switch automatically

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:22.322
sets that mask Their effects must follow from the return being evaluated

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:24.681
rather than from the shape of a software loop

00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:30.876
Consider the smallest possible continuing environment one state one

00:03:30.888 --> 00:03:36.131
action and reward one on every transition Let gamma be point nine Because

00:03:36.142 --> 00:03:40.443
the state never changes the Bellman equation says value equals one plus

00:03:40.539 --> 00:03:44.995
point nine times itself Subtract point nine value from both sides and

00:03:45.007 --> 00:03:49.367
divide The correct value is ten Nothing about this calculation mentions

00:03:49.474 --> 00:03:53.847
episode length Suppose a collector cuts the trajectory after five steps

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.064
At that last recorded transition a correct estimate produces target

00:03:58.076 --> 00:04:03.140
one plus point nine times ten again ten If we treat the cutoff as terminal

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.334
the target suddenly becomes one The physical transition is identical

00:04:07.345 --> 00:04:11.146
to all the others yet our labeling makes it look dramatically worse

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:15.340
There is a useful quantitative extension If a state-independent artificial

00:04:15.352 --> 00:04:19.641
cutoff occurs with probability p at each transition and we mistakenly

00:04:19.653 --> 00:04:24.216
mask it the expected fixed point becomes one divided by one minus gamma

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.898
times one minus p With p equal to point two that is about three point

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:33.163
five seven The interactive lab lets you move both parameters and see

00:04:33.175 --> 00:04:33.950
the bias grow

00:04:35.888 --> 00:04:40.010
Now change the task itself The robot must deliver the cup before a deadline

00:04:40.404 --> 00:04:44.681
A state two steps from success is promising when ten steps remain and

00:04:44.693 --> 00:04:49.125
hopeless when only one step remains If the observation includes only

00:04:49.137 --> 00:04:53.569
cup position these two situations appear identical A value network is

00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:57.739
being asked to assign two different answers to the same input The missing

00:04:57.751 --> 00:05:02.135
variable is remaining time Augment the state with a clock or otherwise

00:05:02.147 --> 00:05:06.174
make time inferable to the policy The finite-horizon value is now a

00:05:06.186 --> 00:05:10.141
function of both state and steps remaining Its recursion decrements

00:05:10.153 --> 00:05:14.657
the clock At zero remaining steps the task really has ended and the

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:19.029
remaining value is zero under this specified reward convention This

00:05:19.041 --> 00:05:22.877
is why always bootstrap and never bootstrap are both incomplete rules

00:05:23.271 --> 00:05:27.345
Ask what process the critic is supposed to represent A continuing task

00:05:27.405 --> 00:05:31.599
artificially sliced for training keeps its tail A deadline-limited task

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.685
includes time and has a genuine boundary The paper's distinction is

00:05:35.697 --> 00:05:39.629
about mathematical consistency between the state the objective and the

00:05:39.641 --> 00:05:43.131
target rather than a universal preference for longer episodes

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.344
Even with the correct mask one implementation detail can quietly undo

00:05:49.356 --> 00:05:53.156
the reasoning Some environment wrappers automatically reset after a

00:05:53.168 --> 00:05:57.267
boundary The next observation they return may be a fresh start while

00:05:57.278 --> 00:06:01.746
the final observation from the old trajectory is stored separately Bootstrapping

00:06:01.758 --> 00:06:05.261
from that fresh start connects two states that were never connected

00:06:05.273 --> 00:06:09.633
by the recorded action Picture the replay tuple as a small causal diagram

00:06:10.027 --> 00:06:13.648
The action leads from the current state to a particular successor and

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.806
the reward belongs to that transition A separate reset arrow must not

00:06:17.818 --> 00:06:22.048
replace the successor arrow Record the actual final observation before

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:26.277
reset and preserve separate termination and truncation information The

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:30.281
precise field names depend on the environment interface In goal-conditioned

00:06:30.292 --> 00:06:34.141
reset-free learning preserve the goal associated with the value update

00:06:34.153 --> 00:06:37.786
too If data collection changes from reaching the shelf to returning

00:06:37.798 --> 00:06:41.980
to the table the old critic's target still concerns its old goal unless

00:06:41.992 --> 00:06:46.686
the algorithm explicitly relabels and recomputes rewards A controller

00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:50.951
handoff changes future behavior off-policy learning specifies how to

00:06:50.963 --> 00:06:54.144
estimate the desired continuation despite that change

00:06:56.093 --> 00:06:59.560
Suppose a robot reaches the shelf once and then keeps holding the cup

00:06:59.572 --> 00:07:04.076
there If the reward is one whenever the cup is on the shelf this behavior

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:08.055
can score extremely well over its continuing lifetime Yet it may have

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:12.558
practiced moving the cup from the table only once A second robot repeatedly

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:17.205
takes the cup down and replaces it sacrificing immediate reward to learn

00:07:17.217 --> 00:07:21.268
the full skill Which robot is better depends on the objective Deployment

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.426
evaluation asks how capable the learned policy is when started from

00:07:25.438 --> 00:07:29.881
the desired distribution Continuing evaluation asks how much reward

00:07:29.893 --> 00:07:34.230
the learning system obtains over the interactions it actually experiences

00:07:34.623 --> 00:07:38.889
Earl makes this distinction explicit Recovery is useful for one objective

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:42.868
even when it temporarily reduces the other For a clear experimental

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.014
report keep evaluation episodes separate from training interventions

00:07:47.407 --> 00:07:50.791
A fresh start used to measure final competence does not automatically

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:55.568
count as a training reset but it should be disclosed Also report environmental

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.143
interactions human resets and elapsed recovery time These quantities

00:08:00.155 --> 00:08:04.289
reveal whether an apparent gain came from better learning easier evaluation

00:08:04.301 --> 00:08:06.934
starts or an uncounted source of assistance

00:08:08.894 --> 00:08:13.433
The phrase reset suggests returning to one exact configuration But learning

00:08:13.445 --> 00:08:17.532
often needs a distribution of useful starting situations Represent the

00:08:17.543 --> 00:08:22.202
states visited by a policy as an occupancy distribution add their visitation

00:08:22.214 --> 00:08:27.158
probabilities across time discount them and normalize A controller changes

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:31.221
not only the next action but the distribution of future data seen by

00:08:31.233 --> 00:08:35.581
the learner When a backward policy runs between task attempts its final

00:08:35.593 --> 00:08:39.716
states become the next forward starts The reset policy is therefore

00:08:39.728 --> 00:08:43.802
also a curriculum generator Sending the robot to the same distant starting

00:08:43.814 --> 00:08:48.389
configuration may be faithful to evaluation yet difficult for discovering

00:08:48.401 --> 00:08:52.940
sparse rewards Sending it to useful intermediate states may create practice

00:08:52.952 --> 00:08:56.943
opportunities that would otherwise be extremely rare There is an important

00:08:56.955 --> 00:09:00.887
distinction between a policy's occupancy and its final-state distribution

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.688
They are related by the dynamics and stopping rule but they are not

00:09:04.699 --> 00:09:09.012
generally equal Later medal will target demonstration-state occupancy

00:09:09.143 --> 00:09:13.230
as a practical way to create useful starts We will keep that modeling

00:09:13.242 --> 00:09:17.090
step visible rather than pretending that matching visits automatically

00:09:17.102 --> 00:09:19.961
proves every handoff distribution is optimal

00:09:21.914 --> 00:09:25.858
A cup can slide to the edge of a table and still be recoverable Once

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:30.159
it falls into a sealed bin outside the robot's reach no available action

00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:34.531
may return it On a state graph the second situation is a region with

00:09:34.626 --> 00:09:39.035
incoming edges but no path back No amount of additional value-function

00:09:39.047 --> 00:09:43.228
training can manufacture a missing physical action Now distinguish that

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.887
structural impossibility from a weak reset policy A robot may currently

00:09:47.899 --> 00:09:51.962
fail to pick up a cup that another policy could retrieve Observed failure

00:09:51.973 --> 00:09:56.215
alone does not prove irreversibility This distinction matters whenever

00:09:56.227 --> 00:10:00.623
an algorithm decides whether to keep trying ask for assistance or veto

00:10:00.635 --> 00:10:04.555
an action before it happens Its judgment is based on limited evidence

00:10:04.948 --> 00:10:09.166
Reset-free methods consequently make different assumptions about recoverability

00:10:09.416 --> 00:10:13.955
supervision and permitted intervention Some reduce manual resets empirically

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.482
Some learn indefinitely in reversible environments A theoretical method

00:10:18.494 --> 00:10:23.141
may allow resets while bounding their growth Read reset-free as a problem

00:10:23.153 --> 00:10:27.144
setting whose operational definition must be stated rather than as an

00:10:27.215 --> 00:10:31.028
automatic promise of zero human assistance in every environment

00:10:32.968 --> 00:10:36.626
We can now organize the literature around questions rather than acronyms

00:10:37.019 --> 00:10:41.082
Leave no Trace asks whether a proposed forward action leaves the reset

00:10:41.094 --> 00:10:45.323
controller enough ability to recover Its critic becomes an action gate

00:10:45.716 --> 00:10:50.255
medal asks which states a backward controller should produce using demonstrations

00:10:50.267 --> 00:10:54.282
to describe a useful distribution These are related questions but they

00:10:54.294 --> 00:10:57.678
change different parts of the system risk asks when to switch between

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:01.693
goals Its surprising choice is to switch early when the current goal

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.185
already looks easy freeing interactions for less familiar regions That

00:11:06.197 --> 00:11:10.093
is a different use of a value-like score from Leave no Trace which aborts

00:11:10.104 --> 00:11:13.691
when recovery looks poor Confusing those two decisions would invert

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:17.503
the intended curriculum The no-regret paper asks what assumptions make

00:11:17.515 --> 00:11:21.566
performance and intervention guarantees possible More Free asks how

00:11:21.578 --> 00:11:25.748
a learned world model can improve both exploration and practice while

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.930
the physical environment continues Throughout the series use the same

00:11:29.941 --> 00:11:34.624
checklist identify the objective the state distribution the return boundary

00:11:34.886 --> 00:11:38.579
and the resource being counted Those four choices explain much of the

00:11:38.591 --> 00:11:40.640
apparent disagreement between methods

00:11:42.591 --> 00:11:46.582
Open the first laboratory and begin with gamma equal to point nine and

00:11:46.594 --> 00:11:50.502
an artificial cutoff probability of zero Both value curves agree at

00:11:50.514 --> 00:11:54.600
ten Increase the false-cutoff probability while keeping the continuing

00:11:54.612 --> 00:11:59.712
task unchanged The incorrect value drops because each extra mask erases

00:11:59.724 --> 00:12:03.500
another expected piece of the future The plot is calculated from the

00:12:03.512 --> 00:12:07.766
fixed-point equations not from a hidden training run Next use the transition

00:12:07.778 --> 00:12:12.591
calculator Enter reward one and successor value ten then toggle true

00:12:12.603 --> 00:12:16.987
termination With termination off the target is ten with termination

00:12:16.999 --> 00:12:21.169
on it is one Toggle only the collector cutoff and watch the correct

00:12:21.181 --> 00:12:25.398
target stay unchanged This isolates semantics from estimation error

00:12:25.792 --> 00:12:29.294
we have deliberately supplied the right successor value so that only

00:12:29.306 --> 00:12:33.286
the boundary decision varies Finally imagine replacing this one-state

00:12:33.298 --> 00:12:37.765
process with a deadline task The calculator alone is now insufficient

00:12:38.027 --> 00:12:41.447
because remaining time belongs in the state and the successor value

00:12:41.459 --> 00:12:45.843
depends on it That limitation is the lesson A formula can be correct

00:12:45.855 --> 00:12:50.466
for one modeled process and wrong for another Before debugging an optimizer

00:12:50.835 --> 00:12:54.147
make sure the quantities in its target describe the process you intended

00:12:56.119 --> 00:12:59.717
We started with an ordinary training convenience and uncovered three

00:12:59.729 --> 00:13:03.971
distinct mathematical objects The return defines what the critic predicts

00:13:04.376 --> 00:13:07.664
The physical transition process defines what the robot can actually

00:13:07.676 --> 00:13:12.608
do The data-collection schedule defines what evidence it sees Many reset-free

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:16.230
learning bugs come from using one of those objects as a substitute for

00:13:16.242 --> 00:13:19.900
another A useful thought experiment is to pause a rollout just before

00:13:19.912 --> 00:13:23.820
a switch Ask whether the old goal could still be pursued from that exact

00:13:23.832 --> 00:13:28.502
state If so its continuation value has not disappeared merely because

00:13:28.514 --> 00:13:31.921
another controller will collect the next action Then ask whether the

00:13:31.933 --> 00:13:35.472
environment itself has been changed by an external intervention That

00:13:35.484 --> 00:13:39.558
is a separate event to record and account for In the next lesson we

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:43.442
will make recovery an explicit learned skill A forward action will have

00:13:43.454 --> 00:13:47.529
to pass a reset critic before being executed We will derive what that

00:13:47.541 --> 00:13:52.044
critic means examine why a high task reward can hide a bad future and

00:13:52.056 --> 00:13:55.678
test how uncertainty changes the permitted region of exploration The

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:58.645
reset button becomes a control problem we can inspect
